<DocLayout title="Security model" description="What PrivyLock protects, how authentication works, and where the boundary ends.">
  <h2>System authentication</h2><p>PrivyLock uses Apple's LocalAuthentication framework and the device-owner authentication policy. macOS handles Touch ID and your Mac login password. PrivyLock receives an authentication result; it never receives, stores, or transmits your fingerprint or password.</p>
  <h2>Reactive app enforcement</h2><p>When an app is locked, PrivyLock requests termination, monitors app launches, and checks running apps with a watchdog. Apps that refuse a normal quit can be force-quit. A protected app may run briefly before it is stopped.</p>
  <h2>Practical limits</h2><ul><li>Protection requires PrivyLock to be running.</li><li>Someone operating as your macOS user can stop PrivyLock or modify its saved configuration without root access.</li><li>Files, notifications, previews, helper processes, and alternative app copies are not isolated.</li><li>Locking can discard unsaved work. Save before locking or enabling automatic locking.</li></ul><p>Use the macOS screen lock and separate user accounts when you need an operating-system access boundary. PrivyLock adds convenience for selected apps within your own session.</p>
  <h2>Data and network behavior</h2><p>The native app stores protected-app identities and settings locally at <code>~/Library/Application Support/AppLock/applock.json</code>. The legacy path preserves configuration across the product rename. Its app-locking and authentication flows do not need a remote service.</p><p>This website fetches the public repository's star count from api.github.com. It uses a five-minute session cache and a static fallback. Release data is fetched during deployment. Fonts are hosted with the site; there is no analytics script or browser-side GitHub token.</p>
  <h2>Report a vulnerability</h2><p>Use <a href="https://github.com/apil-khadka/PrivyLock/security">GitHub's security page</a> for the repository policy and available reporting options. Avoid posting private credentials or exploit details in a public issue.</p>
</DocLayout>
